  <h2 id="remove-or-keep">Remove the element after its exit, or keep it in the DOM</h2>
  <p>
    Both play the entry and the exit of a <a href={glossaryHref('presence')}>presence</a>. The difference is what is left
    once the exit has played. The rule: what a search engine or a screen reader must find stays in the DOM.
  </p>
  <ul class={choices}>
    <li class={card}>
      <h3 class={cardTitle}>
        <ApiName html="remove() after setPresent(false)" react="<Presence>" angular="@if" svelte="{#if} + transition:presence" />
      </h3>
      <p><span class={cardLabel}>Take it when</span> the UI is transient: a toast, a snackbar, a dialog, a list item that is dismissed.</p>
      <p><span class={cardLabel}>Because</span> it is gone once its exit has played, and it has nothing to index.</p>
      <a class={cardLink} href="/motion/presence">Show and hide</a>
    </li>
    <li class={card}>
      <h3 class={cardTitle}>
        <ApiName html="hidden" react="present" angular="[udxMotionPresent]" svelte="present + hidden" />
      </h3>
      <p><span class={cardLabel}>Take it when</span> the content is navigation or text: a menu, an accordion answer, a tab panel.</p>
      <p><span class={cardLabel}>Because</span> the element stays in the DOM, <code>hidden</code> once its exit has played. Its links and text are in the server HTML.</p>
      <a class={cardLink} href="/motion/presence">Keep it in the DOM</a>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">In HTML, <code>hidden</code> is the only trigger: removing an element plays no exit. To remove a transient element, play its exit with <code>getMotion(element, 'presence')?.setPresent(false)</code>, then remove it.</p>
    <p slot="react">In React, <code>&lt;Presence&gt;</code> keeps a child that you stop rendering until its exit has played. <code>present</code> needs no <code>&lt;Presence&gt;</code>: the element is rendered <code>hidden</code> from the server render on.</p>
    <p slot="angular">In Angular, <code>&#64;if</code> removes the element once its exit has played. <code>[udxMotionPresent]</code> keeps it, and Angular sets <code>hidden</code> from the first render.</p>
    <p slot="svelte">In Svelte, <code>transition:presence</code> keeps the element until its exit has played, then <code>&#123;#if&#125;</code> removes it. With <code>present</code>, write <code>hidden=&#123;!open&#125;</code> as well: the server runs no attachment.</p>
  </ForFramework>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-choosing-presence" live={false} />

  <h2 id="preset-or-definition">Take a preset, or define your own motion</h2>
  <ul class={choices}>
    <li class={card}>
      <h3 class={cardTitle}>A preset</h3>
      <p><span class={cardLabel}>Take it when</span> one of the thirteen does what you need: <code>fade</code>, <code>slide</code>, <code>scale</code>, <code>cascade</code>, <code>draw</code>, <code>press</code>, <code>shake</code>, <code>rise</code>, <code>parallax</code>, <code>reflow</code>, <code>rotate</code>, <code>breathe</code>, <code>blink</code>.</p>
      <p><span class={cardLabel}>Because</span> importing <code>@udixio/motion/presets</code> defines them all. You name one and write nothing else.</p>
      <a class={cardLink} href="/motion/presets">Presets and verbs</a>
    </li>
    <li class={card}>
      <h3 class={cardTitle}>defineMotion()</h3>
      <p><span class={cardLabel}>Take it when</span> you need other keyframes, another timing, or an option a preset does not set: <code>stagger</code>, <code>origin</code>, <code>exit</code>, <code>repeat</code>, <code>range</code>, an essential loop.</p>
      <p><span class={cardLabel}>Because</span> a preset is only a definition with a name. Yours works the same way.</p>
      <a class={cardLink} href="/motion/presets">Define a motion</a>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <Code data-code={ownDefinition} data-language="ts" />
  <p>Name it well:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Use kebab-case, and name what the motion is for: <code>toast-enter</code>, <code>badge-pop</code>, not <code>up-64</code>.</li>
    <li>Do not reuse a preset's name. Once the presets are imported, defining <code>slide</code> with other keyframes throws. If you define it first, importing the presets throws.</li>
    <li>Reserved names are refused: <code>enter</code>, <code>exit</code>, <code>in</code>, <code>out</code>, <code>transition</code>, <code>animate</code>, <code>initial</code>, <code>AnimatePresence</code>, and the prefix <code>view-transition-</code>.</li>
    <li>
      Define it once, in a module that runs before the element is bound. You can pass the reference that
      <code>defineMotion()</code> returns in place of the name:
      <ApiName html={'data-motion="toast-enter"'} react="name={toastEnter}" angular={'[udxMotion]="toastEnter"'} svelte="motion(toastEnter)" />.
      The import then makes sure the definition has run.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="loops">Loop: the verb, animateStyle, or a time value</h2>
  <p>Three parts of the library repeat forever. They differ by what they move and by who pauses them.</p>
  <ul class={choices}>
    <li class={card}>
      <h3 class={cardTitle}>verb: 'loop'</h3>
      <p><span class={cardLabel}>Take it when</span> an element spins, breathes or blinks with the motion fields: decoration, or a loop that is the content, such as an indeterminate bar.</p>
      <p><span class={cardLabel}>Because</span> it pauses out of view and in a hidden tab, stays still under reduced motion, and states how a visitor pauses it (WCAG 2.2.2).</p>
      <a class={cardLink} href="/motion/loop">Loop, and pause it</a>
    </li>
    <li class={card}>
      <h3 class={cardTitle}>iterations: Infinity</h3>
      <p><span class={cardLabel}>Take it when</span> one element loops a CSS property that a motion cannot, such as a <code>box-shadow</code>.</p>
      <p><span class={cardLabel}>Because</span> <code>animateStyle()</code> takes any property. You hold the handle: you pause it and cancel it.</p>
      <a class={cardLink} href="/motion/animate">Animate any CSS</a>
    </li>
    <li class={card}>
      <h3 class={cardTitle}>source: 'time'</h3>
      <p><span class={cardLabel}>Take it when</span> you need a number that cycles, and your CSS decides what it drives: a hue, a gradient angle, several elements at once.</p>
      <p><span class={cardLabel}>Because</span> a <a href={glossaryHref('value')}>value</a> writes a CSS variable and animates nothing itself. It stops out of view, in a hidden tab, and under reduced motion.</p>
      <a class={cardLink} href="/motion/values">Values</a>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <ul>
    <li>All three take a curve, <code>&#123; duration, easing &#125;</code>: the duration of one cycle is a token or milliseconds.</li>
    <li>A time value has no pause of its own: <code>destroy()</code> on its binding stops it. If it runs beside other content for more than five seconds, give the page a control that destroys it and binds it again.</li>
    <li>
      An <code>animateStyle()</code> loop reads reduced motion once, when you call it. It does not pause out of view. If it
      runs for long, play it only while its element is active:
    </li>
  </ul>
  <Code data-code={pauseByHand} data-language="ts" />

  <h2 id="scroll">Scroll: reveal, scrub, scrollTo, or a scroll value</h2>
  <p>Four parts of the library deal with the scroll. Ask yourself whether the scroll starts the motion, drives it, or is the motion.</p>
  <ul class={choices}>
    <li class={card}>
      <h3 class={cardTitle}>verb: 'reveal'</h3>
      <p><span class={cardLabel}>Take it when</span> an element enters as it scrolls into view: once, or on each entry with <code>repeat: 'always'</code>.</p>
      <p><span class={cardLabel}>Because</span> the view starts it, and its timing plays it: scrolling faster does not make it faster.</p>
      <a class={cardLink} href="/motion/reveal">Reveal on scroll</a>
    </li>
    <li class={card}>
      <h3 class={cardTitle}>verb: 'scrub'</h3>
      <p><span class={cardLabel}>Take it when</span> an element moves with the scroll position, forward and backward, such as a parallax.</p>
      <p><span class={cardLabel}>Because</span> the element's crossing of the view drives its keyframes. It has no timing. Without a view timeline, the element keeps its stylesheet state.</p>
      <a class={cardLink} href="/motion/scrub">Link to the scroll</a>
    </li>
    <li class={card}>
      <h3 class={cardTitle}>scrollTo()</h3>
      <p><span class={cardLabel}>Take it when</span> a click must move the scroll: back to the top, to a section, to the next card of a box.</p>
      <p><span class={cardLabel}>Because</span> it scrolls over a spring, follows a layout that moves, and stops when the visitor scrolls.</p>
      <a class={cardLink} href="/motion/scroll">Scroll to a target</a>
    </li>
    <li class={card}>
      <h3 class={cardTitle}>source: 'scroll'</h3>
      <p><span class={cardLabel}>Take it when</span> a number must follow how far the page has scrolled, such as a reading bar, and drive any CSS.</p>
      <p><span class={cardLabel}>Because</span> it writes the progress of the window's scroll, from 0 to 1, to a CSS variable. It follows the window only.</p>
      <a class={cardLink} href="/motion/values">Values</a>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If a box scrolls inside the page, a scroll value does not follow it: set a <code>state</code> value from the box's
    own scroll handler. If an element must change a colour as it crosses the view, neither a scrub nor a scroll value
    does it: a scrub moves motion fields only, and a scroll value follows the whole page.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cascade">Cascade: the stagger option, or the stagger functions</h2>
  <ul class={choices}>
    <li class={card}>
      <h3 class={cardTitle}>stagger: 'normal'</h3>
      <p><span class={cardLabel}>Take it when</span> the items of a list enter, leave or reveal together, with a motion of the verbs <code>appear</code>, <code>disappear</code> or <code>reveal</code>.</p>
      <p><span class={cardLabel}>Because</span> the runtime groups the items of one definition that start in the same task, in the same <a href={glossaryHref('scope')}>scope</a>, ranks them in visual order from <code>origin</code>, and drops the delays under reduced motion.</p>
      <a class={cardLink} href="/motion/lists">Make a list appear</a>
    </li>
    <li class={card}>
      <h3 class={cardTitle}>staggerDelay()</h3>
      <p><span class={cardLabel}>Take it when</span> you cascade your own code: <code>animateStyle()</code> calls, springs, CSS transition delays.</p>
      <p><span class={cardLabel}>Because</span> <code>staggerDelay(rank, step, max)</code> returns <code>min(rank × step, max)</code>, and <code>staggerStep()</code> and <code>staggerCap()</code> read the same tokens as the runtime.</p>
      <a class={cardLink} href="/motion/animate">Stagger a cascade</a>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <Code data-code={cascades} data-language="ts" />
  <p>
    With the functions, the rank is yours: the DOM order, unless you compute another. The delays stay under reduced
    motion: <code>animateStyle()</code> ignores them, since it shows its frame at once, but a CSS transition does not.
  </p>

  <h2 id="replay">Replay a motion on demand</h2>
  <p>
    Most motions start by themselves. When you must ask for one, there is one path, <code>play()</code>, and what it does
    depends on the <a href={glossaryHref('kind')}>kind</a> it plays:
  </p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th class={th}>Kind</th><th class={th}>What <code>play()</code> does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><a href={glossaryHref('emphasis')}>Emphasis</a></td><td>Replays it from its first frame.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Reveal</td><td>Puts the element back in its first frame, and plays the reveal again.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Layout</td><td>Compares the layout now, and moves the element: what an inline style needs, since the layout does not observe it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Loop</td><td>Resumes a loop that you paused.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Scrub</td><td>Nothing: the scroll drives it. Development warns once.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Presence</td><td>Nothing: a presence is shown or hidden, with <ApiName html="hidden" react="present" angular="[udxMotionPresent]" svelte="present" /> or <code>setPresent()</code>. Development reports it once.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <ForFramework>
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <p>
        Pass the value that changes as <code>state</code>: each new value calls <code>play()</code> on the first listed
        motion that is not a presence, once React has applied the render. From a control, call
        <code>play(kind?)</code> from <code>motionRef</code> on <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code>, or from <code>useMotion()</code>.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="angular">
      <p>
        Bind the value that changes to <code>[udxMotionState]</code>: each new value calls <code>play()</code> on the first
        listed motion that is not a presence, once the host has rendered. From a control, call <code>play(kind?)</code> on
        the directive, taken as <code>#m="udxMotion"</code>.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="svelte">
      <p>
        Pass the value that changes as the <code>state</code> option: each new value calls <code>play()</code> on the first
        listed motion that is not a presence, once Svelte has updated the element. From a control, bind the element with
        <code>bind:this</code> and call <code>getMotion(element, kind)?.play()</code>.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="html">
      <p>
        Change <code>data-motion-state</code>: each new value calls <code>play()</code> on the first motion listed in
        <code>data-motion</code> that is not a presence. From script, call <code>getMotion(element, kind)?.play()</code>.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
  </ForFramework>
  <p>The example presses the button, and moves the card after its new width, from one value:</p>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-choosing-replay" live={false} />
</MotionPage>
